  "text_md": "![](<4_pg380_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<4_pg380_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV VARIABLES AND LIMITS\n\n400. Functions (§ 292) are usually denoted by symbols of\n\nthe form f(x), P(@), F(@), etc., and are read f function of a,\n\nP function of x, etc.\n\nThus, f(x ) comprises all expressions which involve the letter «, and it\n\nmay represent in one discussion 322+ 2+ 1, in another Vz, in a third 4x + 2%3, etc.\n\n401. If f(x) is known in any particular discussion, f(a) is\n\nformed by substituting a in place of a.\n\nE.g. If S(®) = 20742443,\n\nthen f8)=2-87+2-843=27,\n\nS(a)= 2024+ 2a+4+8, f0)=0+0+4+3=8, ete.\n\n- 402. If y= f(x), « is called the independent variable, and y\n\nthe dependent variable.\n\n- 403. Similarly, /(#, 7) denotes a function of two independent\n\n\nvariables. Thus, 2?+3ay+y , or Va+vVy, or zi may be\n\nrepresented by the symbol /(a, y). y\n\nee AL SQ, y= +4, f(2,3)= 28 + 2=10.\n\nEXERCISE 142\n\n- 1. If f(x) =2?+3e+42, find f(1), f0), f( 1).\n- 2. If f(x) =5e 2e 3, find f(2), f(a), (0).\n- 3. It f(x) =4, find (0), f( 1), £G).\n\n\n364\n\n",
